    gemini: `### Section 1 — Role
You are a senior web designer with 12+ years of experience building healthcare authority websites. Your design philosophy: "Data-driven design creates trust." You reject overly flashy design elements that detract from user focus. Every page serves one conversion goal: "Encourage appointment bookings."

### Section 2 — Application Overview
The product type is a Global Hospital Authority Portal. User personas include: 
1. "Dr. Emily Chen, Cardiologist, needs to showcase her successful patient outcomes." 
2. "John Smith, prospective patient, needs reliable information to determine if the clinic is right for his heart procedure." 
3. "Nurse Sarah Jones, clinic administrator, needs to streamline the appointment scheduling process." 
All pages to build: Home, About Us, Services, Doctor Profiles, Patient Resources, Contact Us. The primary conversion event is appointment booking.

### Section 3 — Brand Voice & Mood
Aesthetic: "Institutional Minimalism". If Cleveland Clinic were a building, it would be a modern, glass-walled hospital showcasing transparency and care. Adjectives: "Trustworthy, Professional, Data-driven." Anti-quality to avoid: "Overly casual." Define copy tone: concise sentences, formal structure, clear CTAs like "Book Your Appointment Today!" Vibe word: "Authority."

### Section 4 — Core Features & Functionality
1. Doctor Profiles – Grid layout with profile image, name, specialty, and "View Profile" button leading to detailed bios.
2. Appointment Scheduling – Multi-step form with date picker, insurance selector, and inline validation.
3. Patient Resources – Downloadable PDF resources with clear "Download Now" buttons.
4. Testimonials Carousel – Rotating quotes from patients, with "Read More" links to full stories.
5. FAQs Section – Accordion-style dropdowns for questions, with "Ask a Question" button for further inquiries.
6. Live Chat Support – Chat widget in bottom corner for real-time assistance with "Chat Now" button.

### Section 5 — Design Specifications
- Color palette: 
  - Primary: #0468CD (action) 
  - Secondary: #FFFFFF (background) 
  - Accent: #F2F2F2 (muted background) 
  - Text: #333333 (primary text) 
  - Muted: #B0B0B0 (secondary text)
- Typography: 
  - h1: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem), font-family: 'Arial', weight: 700
  - h2: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.5rem), font-family: 'Arial', weight: 600
  - body: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 1.125rem), font-family: 'Arial', weight: 400
- Spacing: 8px base, 4/8/16/24/32/48/64 scale
- Border radius: 4px for buttons, 8px for cards, 2px for inputs
- Responsive: mobile-first breakpoints (sm:640, md:768, lg:1024, xl:1280)
- Accessibility: WCAG 2.1 AA with contrast ratios above 4.5:1, focus-visible outlines, aria labels for all interactive elements
- Motion: Fade-in duration 300ms, easing: ease-in-out, prefers-reduced-motion: opacity: 1 instant.
  
### Section 6 — Structure
Page: Home
1. Hero – headline "Your Trusted Heart Care Provider", subhead, primary CTA button "Book Your Appointment", background treatment with a calming image.
2. Metrics Showcase – Display key metrics (e.g., "35,000+ Heart Procedures"), styled as a grid with icons.
3. Featured Services – 3-column layout with service title, short description, and "Learn More" button.
4. Testimonials Section – Quotes from patients with images, "Read More" links.
5. Call to Action – "Schedule Your Consultation Today" with a prominent button.

Page: About Us
1. Mission Statement – Headline, paragraph of text.
2. Leadership Team – Grid of team members with names, titles, and "View Profile" links.
3. History Timeline – Vertical timeline with key milestones.

Page: Services
1. Service Categories – Grid of categories with icons and "Explore" buttons.
2. Detailed Service Descriptions – Accordion for each service with "Learn More" links.

Page: Doctor Profiles
1. Search Bar – Input for searching doctors.
2. Doctor List – Card layout with profile image, name, specialty, "View Profile" buttons.

Page: Patient Resources
1. Resource Categories – Grid layout with downloadable resources.
2. FAQs – Accordion style for common questions.

Page: Contact Us
1. Contact Form – Fields for name, email, message, and "Submit" button.
2. Location Map – Embedded Google Map with location marker.

### Section 7 — Technical Specifications
Framework and tooling: Next.js 14 App Router, TypeScript strict mode, Tailwind CSS v4. State management approach: React Context API for shared state. Component patterns: server components for static data, client components for interactive elements. Image optimization strategy using Next.js Image component. SEO defaults: metadata API for title and description, OpenGraph for social sharing.

### Section 8 — Implementation Steps
Step 1: Scaffold Next.js 14 project with TypeScript + Tailwind. (Foundation — everything depends on this.)
Step 2: Create design tokens in tailwind.config.ts — colors, fonts, spacing. (All components reference these.)
Step 3: Build layout shell — Nav + Footer + page wrapper. (Shared across all pages.)
Step 4: Develop shared components — Button, Card, Input, Badge with variant props. (Reusability across pages.)
Step 5: Implement page compositions — integrate sections defined in structure for each page. (Final assembly of the site.)

### Section 9 — User Experience
Define primary user goal: "Find a suitable doctor and book an appointment." Define secondary goal: "Access valuable patient resources." UX principles: 
1. Progressive disclosure — show only essential information first, reveal more upon interaction.
2. Instant feedback — every interaction produces a visible response within 100ms.
3. Consistent navigation — ensure users can easily move between sections without confusion.

### Section 10 — Constraints
NEVER use auto-playing videos — instead use a prominent "Watch Our Introduction" button because unexpected audio can increase bounce rate by 40%. 
NEVER clutter the homepage with too much text — instead use concise metrics and visuals to engage users quickly because it helps maintain user focus. 
NEVER use carousels for testimonials — instead use static sections with clear quotes because carousels have low interaction rates. 
NEVER rely solely on images for important information — instead provide text alternatives because accessibility improves user experience for all.`,
